      en: "The Yergeni Upland is a hilly region in southwestern Russia, east of the Volga River and north of the Caucasus. It stretches roughly 350 km from northwest to southeast. Average elevations range from 100 to 200 m, with maximum around 220 m. The landscape consists of low hills and shallow valleys, covered by steppe vegetation. The upland is underlain by Tertiary and Quaternary sediments, including sands, clays, and carbonates. Agriculture is prominent, with wheat and sunflowers as main crops. The Yergeni drains into the Volga and Manych rivers. The climate is dry continental, with 300–400 mm annual precipitation.",
