      en: "The Turgai Hollow is a depression about 600 km long in northwestern Kazakhstan and southwestern Russia, connecting the Urals with the Aral Sea. Its width ranges from 20 to 200 km. Elevations are mostly between 100 and 200 m. The hollow formed during the Jurassic and Cretaceous periods and is filled with Mesozoic-Cenozoic sediments. The Turgai River once flowed through it but is now largely dry. The hollow is an important biogeographic barrier and migration route for steppe animals. Vegetation is typical dry steppe with feather grass and wormwood. The climate is continental, with hot summers and cold winters. The area is used for extensive livestock grazing.",
